*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.workflow.js text eol=lf

# ENG-95683 review — the JS toolchain is LF-ONLY, and this is a correctness rule, not a style one.
# `scripts/build-workflows.mjs` inlines `_workflow-core/*.mjs` into `*.workflow.js` (already pinned LF above) and
# then byte-compares the result. On a CRLF checkout the sources arrive CRLF, the generated file is pinned LF, and
# `--check` reports EVERY line as drifted — which makes the one gate protecting the generated artifacts unreadable
# rather than merely noisy, and hides real drift inside the noise (that is exactly how the blank-line drift this
# PR removes survived). `run-workflow-parity.mjs` fails the same way: it slices the shipped script and evaluates
# it, and CRLF breaks the slice with `SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'`.
# Every blob under these two rules is ALREADY stored LF, so for them this changes only what lands in a working
# tree. There is ONE exception, and it is exactly the case where the rule would be unsafe — see `fixtures` below.
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf

# Review (m-dymytrova) — the captured Classic schema fixtures are EXEMPT. This is data integrity, not style.
# Seven of them (contract/ContractInInvoice, ContractInOrder, CoreContracts, DocumentInContract, SalesContracts,
# WorkCompliance, WorkOverride) are stored with DOUBLE-CR line endings (CR CR LF) — genuinely what the stand
# emitted. Git classifies them `i/-text`, because the lone CRs defeat its text detection, so until the `*.js` rule
# above they had never been reached by any attribute. Declaring them `text` OVERRIDES that detection and makes Git
# want to halve every CR CR LF to CR LF: 2401 lines across the 7 files, which a plain `git add -A` is enough to
# stage. These fixtures are the input `run.mjs` feeds straight to `parseSchema` across 122 checks, and because the
# engine normalizes line endings before parsing, that rewrite leaves the goldens GREEN — the corpus would quietly
# stop being the bytes the stand produced, with no failing test to notice it. `-text` opts them out of every EOL
# conversion, which is also how they behaved before the rules above existed.
engine-tests/classic-to-freedom/fixtures/** -text
